-- Per-skill/spell help topics (user 2026-07-18: "also no spell
-- helpfiles? that was my instruction. help files for all skills/spells
-- along with required components" -- a correction: the earlier
-- inline-note-in-`skills` approach was NOT what was asked for, this
-- generates a real `help <name>` topic for every entry in skill.c's
-- SKILLS[] roster instead). Machine-generated -- a one-shot Python
-- script (not committed to the repo; regenerate by hand if skill.c's
-- roster changes significantly) parsed SKILLS[]'s own
-- name/class/tier/level/description fields directly, so this can't
-- drift out of sync with the roster by hand-transcription error.
-- One row per unique skill/spell NAME (25 names are shared across more
-- than one class -- mostly identical weapon-proficiency rows, plus a
-- handful of same-named Cleric/Druid prayers-and-spells like "cure
-- poison" -- merged into a single topic listing every class that has
-- it, rather than colliding on help_topic's `name` primary key).
--
-- REDESIGNED 2026-07-22 (user wireframe, using "gust" as the worked
-- example): a real `Usage: cast/pray <name> [<target>]` leading line
-- (parsed by cmd_help.c's existing generic Usage-stripping logic, same
-- as any real command topic) whenever the spell is cast/pray-reachable
-- -- omitted for still-placeholder physical skills, which have nothing
-- to show; a bare `Classes:` list with NO tier/level parenthetical
-- (that number now lives in the new `Approx. Level:` footer instead);
-- `Requires:` names the REAL specific reagent a spell traditionally
-- used, for every spell the upstream source actually assigned one to.
-- Two follow-up asks drove this to its final form: (1) "this should
-- list the actual component required for the spell" -- first pass used
-- `obj/obj_component.h`'s `const int COMP_<SPELL> = <vnum>;` table (56
-- matches); (2) "is there a list of components to spells from the code
-- we could map" -- yes, a far more complete one: `misc/spell_num.cc`'s
-- `mapFileToSpellnum()` (~500 entries, file_to_spell.py) maps every
-- component object's raw file-format "value 3" field -- which Tobin's
-- own `obj.val2` preserves verbatim -- to its real spell. This
-- generator now queries EVERY real "component"-keyword row in Tobin's
-- own seeded `obj` table live, joins each one's `val2` through that
-- table, and keeps the lowest-vnum match per spell (same "lowest vnum
-- wins" precedent the original's own `CompInfo.comp_num` tracking
-- uses) -- far more real matches than the original 56-entry hand
-- curation, and confirmed live against Tobin's real seeded rows, not
-- guessed. Every OTHER Mage/Druid spell (the upstream never assigned
-- these one either) keeps the honest generic phrasing (`a pouch of
-- spell components` -- a real, commonly-seeded row, not a one-off)
-- rather than fabricating a specific item. Cleric's holy-symbol
-- Requires stays generic throughout (`a wooden holy symbol`) -- the
-- upstream's own component system is Wizardry/material-reagent-
-- specific, Ritualism's holy symbols were never assigned individual
-- per-prayer items the same way. A new `Discipline:` footer line
-- (skill/spell topics only, shown only when the upstream source
-- actually has one) reports the real `misc/spell_info.cc`
-- `discArray[]` `start` value -- a genuine 0-100 threshold against
-- that spell's OWN discipline track in the original (user: "along with
-- the % of discipline needed to gain the spell/skill"). Shown as-is,
-- per the user's own explicit choice when asked how to reconcile it
-- with Tobin's simplified single basic_disc_pct/advanced_disc_pct pair
-- (the original splits casters into several separate per-school
-- disciplines -- Air/Fire/Water/Mage/Ritualism/... -- that Tobin
-- collapsed into one each; a shown "Discipline: 26%" is against that
-- spell's own unported discipline, not literally Tobin's own
-- basic_disc_pct). The description
-- itself is auto-elaborated with a targeting sentence (area-effect vs.
-- single-target vs. self-only vs. "not wired to a real effect yet"),
-- derived from EXACTLY the same substring categorization cmd_cast.c's
-- task_cast() itself uses to dispatch the real effect, so the help
-- text can never silently drift out of sync with what a spell actually
-- does. A name that's already a real dispatch-table command (disarm,
-- riding -> `ride`, sign -> `sign`, steal, peek, repair, the set-trap
-- variants -> `settrap`, disarm trap -> `disarmtrap`, ...) generates a
-- row that says so plainly -- inert in practice, since help_topic.sql's
-- own real entry for that name is applied first and wins (`ON DUPLICATE
-- KEY UPDATE name=name` is a no-op on the second insert), but accurate
-- rather than misleading if anyone ever reads this file directly.
-- "disease" is deliberately EXCLUDED here -- it collides with an
-- existing general-mechanic topic (drink a puddle -> catch a disease --
-- see below) that predates this session's Cleric "disease" prayer;
-- handled by hand-appending to that existing topic instead of fighting
-- over one name.
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE `body` = IF(`updated_by` = 'seed', VALUES(`body`), `body`);
-- topic entirely (2026-08-05 TODO follow-up: 'players currently only
-- discover you need a component/holy symbol by trying to cast and
-- getting refused' -- turned out the underlying help_topic generator
-- system already existed since 2026-07-22 above, but these six were
-- added to skill.c's roster afterward and never got a matching row).
-- Same hand-authored format/conventions as the generated rows above --
-- Requires/Approx. Level/Discipline sourced the same way (skill.c for
-- level/classes, sneezymud-master/code/code/misc/spell_info.cc's real
-- discArray[] START_ values for Discipline where a real upstream entry
-- exists; protection from earth/inferno/sterilize/stupidity all have
-- one, summon swarm/animal companion are Tobin-original mechanics with
-- no upstream spellInfo entry to cite, so their Discipline line is
-- omitted, same precedent the generator itself used).
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE `body` = IF(`updated_by` = 'seed', VALUES(`body`), `body`);
 'Usage: cast death mist\n\nA high Druid working of rot and plague. You breathe out a chilling green mist that rolls across the whole room, infecting every creature caught in it -- friend and foe alike, save your own groupmates -- with a wasting syphilitic sickness that saps their health over time. Handle it with care: some of the mist can curl back over you and leave you infected as well. Ported from real SneezyMUD''s Shaman death mist, folded onto the Druid.\nRelated: cure disease creeping doom\nApprox. Level: 45\nClasses: Druid',
 'system')
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE body=VALUES(body), updated_by=VALUES(updated_by);
